Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?P18:=20daemon=20=E2=80=94=20respond=20with=20er?=
 =?UTF-8?q?ror=20frame=20if=20client=20cwd=20is=20unreachable?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Bug found by sweeping patch 0010: handleOneClient silently ignored
chdir() failures. If the client's cwd was deleted or has no exec perm,
the dispatcher ran from the daemon's cwd; the user got a mysterious
'board file does not exist' instead of a real diagnostic.

Fix: send an ERR_UNKNOWN response frame with a specific error message
whenever ::chdir fails, and skip the dispatch. prevCwd is still freed
before returning.
---
 kicad/cli/command_daemon.cpp | 20 +++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/kicad/cli/command_daemon.cpp b/kicad/cli/command_daemon.cpp
index fbb39011..b1e95fef 100644
--- a/kicad/cli/command_daemon.cpp
+++ b/kicad/cli/command_daemon.cpp
@@ -512,11 +512,25 @@ static void handleOneClient( int aClientFd )
         argvC.push_back( s.data() );
 
     // Change to the client's cwd so relative paths (input .kicad_pcb,
-    // -o output/) resolve where the caller expected.
+    // -o output/) resolve where the caller expected. If the client's cwd
+    // is inaccessible (deleted, no perm), respond with a clear error
+    // instead of silently running from the daemon's cwd — the alternative
+    // was a confusing "board file does not exist" from the dispatcher.
     char* prevCwd = ::getcwd( nullptr, 0 );
 
-    if( !req.cwd.empty() )
-        (void) ::chdir( req.cwd.c_str() );
+    if( !req.cwd.empty() && ::chdir( req.cwd.c_str() ) != 0 )
+    {
+        std::string err = "daemon: cannot chdir to client cwd '";
+        err += req.cwd;
+        err += "': ";
+        err += ::strerror( errno );
+        err += '\n';
+
+        writeResponse( aClientFd, CLI::EXIT_CODES::ERR_UNKNOWN, /*stdout=*/{}, err );
+        std::free( prevCwd );
+        ::close( aClientFd );
+        return;
+    }
 
     // Snapshot process-global mutable state that CLI commands might set
     // and forget to restore. Destructor at scope exit puts it back so the
